/* DOXA theme -- Phase 1 slice 2 scope only (see README's status table: full
   spec lives in the private vault, this file implements the approved
   load-bearing subset).

   Tokens: dark surface ramp #171512 (screen) -> #1D1B17 (dimmer step,
   system/doxa-chrome) -> #221F1A (blocks) -> #2A251E (nested chips) ->
   #3A3429 (borders, top of the ramp); primary #D97757 (Claude orange);
   generous padding; warm off-white text (#F2E9DD body / #D8CDBB secondary
   / #8A8073 dim). The block accent is the literal "▎" glyph doxa/app.py's
   TurnBlock/SystemBlock prefix their titles with -- a left-rule character,
   not a CSS border trick, so it reads identically whether or not the
   terminal's border-drawing glyphs render cleanly.

   v0.13.0 visual restyle: round borders remain on genuinely BOXED chrome
   -- ToolChip (and its ToolCallsSection wrapper), modals, panels, the
   prompt input -- but the transcript's own turns and system lines carry
   their role as a background TINT instead of a border (see the TurnBlock/
   SystemBlock rules below for the full rationale). No new colors were
   added for this -- every tint used is already a stop on the ramp above.

   NOTHING ANIMATES. No transitions, no pulsing borders, no spinners: the
   in-flight turn marker (`.thinking` below) is a static Static, having
   replaced a LoadingIndicator whose 16 Hz auto-refresh was CPU spent on
   reassurance. The only interval left anywhere is Textual's own 2 Hz
   caret blink on the focused prompt input, which is load-bearing -- it is
   how you find the caret -- and runs on exactly one widget.

   $doxa-base (v0.29.0, the `background` setting): every selector below
   that used to hardcode the ramp's own #171512 now reads $doxa-base
   instead -- DoxaApp.get_theme_variable_defaults resolves it to the
   literal "#171512" (opaque, the default -- byte-identical to every
   release before it), or, for "transparent", the CSS keyword
   "ansi_default" -- which Rich renders as the raw SGR "default background"
   reset (ESC[49m) instead of any RGB paint, letting an already-transparent
   terminal show through. This requires App.ansi_color = True to reach the terminal
   unconverted (DoxaApp sets it alongside the variable); without it,
   Textual's own ANSIToTruecolor filter silently rewrites ansi_default
   into an approximated opaque RGB, which would paint instead of clearing.
   Only the BASE token moved -- every other rung of the ramp (#1D1B17
   dimmer/system, #221F1A raised, #2A251E chips, #3A3429 borders) stays a
   literal hex, unconditionally painted, so role tints keep reading as
   distinct steps against whatever the terminal shows through as. The five
   modal washes (CloseWithTurnRunning, CompactConfirm, CommandPalette,
   SettingsScreen, SetupScreen -- all `#171512 60%`) are the deliberate
   exception: Rich's "default" color has no RGB for Textual to blend a
   percentage against, so pairing ansi_default with an alpha suffix is a
   no-op that would drop the dimming wash entirely -- those five keep the
   literal hex regardless of the setting, on purpose. */

Screen {
    background: $doxa-base;
    color: #F2E9DD;
    /* Two layers, not one: "base" is everything that already existed
       (tabs, panes, docked panels), "overlay" is paint-only chrome that
       must never take space away from it. The clock chip (item M) is the
       one thing on "overlay" today -- see #doxa-clock below. */
    layers: base overlay;
}

/* Session tabs (Phase 3): the TabbedContent hosts one SessionPane per
   session; both default to height:auto, which would collapse the panes'
   1fr block list -- pin the whole tab system to the screen. The tab strip
   itself rides the surface ramp; the active tab gets the orange accent. */
#session-tabs {
    height: 1fr;
}

SessionPane {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 0;
}

#session-tabs ContentTabs {
    background: $doxa-base;
    color: #D8CDBB;
}

#session-tabs Tab {
    color: #D8CDBB;
}

#session-tabs Tab.-active {
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* Per-tab status, composed with -active rather than fighting it: a tab
   answers "which session, and what is it doing right now" at a glance
   without opening it. Single-class rules cascade in the order below (equal
   selector specificity, later wins) so a tab carrying more than one status
   class at once resolves to the state that matters most: done-unseen <
   staged < working < attention. -active is left OUT of that ladder -- its
   own rule above still wins the FOREGROUND color on a plain active tab --
   and the two compound rules at the end explicitly restore the
   working/attention color on top of it, because those two are the ones
   worth seeing even on the tab you are already looking at (see
   doxa/app.py's SessionPane._set_tab_class and the state-precedence note
   in the tab status work item).

   done-unseen and staged never appear on the active tab (app-level
   invariant: both are cleared the moment a tab activates), so neither
   needs a compound rule of its own -- there is nothing for them to lose a
   fight with. */
#session-tabs Tab.-done-unseen {
    color: #6FCF97;
}

/* Staged memory proposals from the background reviewer (v0.31.0). A
   STEADY tint, never a blink: blinking belongs to -attention, which means
   "this session is stopped until you answer". A staged proposal stops
   nothing, expires never, and reaches curated memory only through an
   explicit human approval -- so it gets the calmest signal on this ladder
   that is still distinct from "a turn finished". Muted violet: nowhere
   near the green of done-unseen or the amber of working, so a tab
   carrying it is not misread as either, and low-saturation enough that a
   whole strip of them is not a wall of noise. */
#session-tabs Tab.-staged {
    color: #A98FD1;
}

#session-tabs Tab.-working {
    color: #E0A83C;
}

#session-tabs Tab.-attention {
    color: #F2E9DD;
    background: #B23B32;
    text-style: bold;
}

#session-tabs Tab.-active.-working {
    color: #E0A83C;
    text-style: bold;
}

#session-tabs Tab.-active.-attention {
    color: #F2E9DD;
    background: #B23B32;
    text-style: bold;
}

#session-tabs Underline > .underline--bar {
    color: #D97757;
    background: #3A3429;
}

/* The upper-right clock (item M). `layer: overlay` + `dock: right` +
   `width: auto` is deliberate as a set, not any one of them alone:
   docking a widget on the SAME layer as the tab bar reserves its column
   for the full screen height (measured -- a docked sidebar-style
   reservation, not just its own row), which would narrow every pane's
   body permanently for two rows' worth of clock. On its own layer, dock
   only affects arrangement WITHIN that layer, so the tab bar and every
   pane keep their full width and are never displaced or resized; the
   clock simply paints on top of whatever is at the top-right corner
   (height 2, matching Tabs' own default height) -- covering a tab label
   that reaches that far is the accepted trade, never reflowing one.
   `width: auto` keeps its footprint (and therefore its mouse hit box) to
   exactly its own text, so clicking a tab anywhere the clock isn't
   painted still reaches the tab underneath. */
#doxa-clock {
    layer: overlay;
    dock: right;
    width: auto;
    height: 2;
    content-align: center middle;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    background: $doxa-base;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* Ctrl+Q with a turn running: the one close that asks. Same modal wash and
   surface ramp as the palette and the settings screen. */
CloseWithTurnRunning {
    align: center middle;
    background: #171512 60%;
}

#close-confirm {
    width: 64;
    height: auto;
    background: #221F1A;
    border: round #3A3429;
    padding: 1 2;
}

#close-confirm-title {
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
    padding-bottom: 1;
}

#close-confirm-body {
    color: #D8CDBB;
}

/* `height: auto`, NOT `height: 1` (v0.28.0, reported against the twin rule
   below: "no button to continue, no OK"). Textual's box model is
   border-box, so a padded row spends its declared height on the padding
   FIRST: `height: 1` plus `padding-top: 1` left the content box exactly 0
   rows tall and the Statics inside rendered at Size(width=0, height=0) --
   present in the DOM, clickable in theory, invisible and unhittable on
   screen. `auto` sizes to padding PLUS content, so the row is 2 rows and
   the buttons are real. */
#close-confirm-buttons {
    height: auto;
    padding-top: 1;
}

#close-confirm-buttons > Static {
    width: auto;
    padding-right: 2;
    color: #F2E9DD;
}

/* ctx% chip's compact confirm (a real defect fix -- see CompactConfirm's
   own docstring): SAME modal wash and surface ramp as CloseWithTurnRunning
   above, just two doors instead of three. */
CompactConfirm {
    align: center middle;
    background: #171512 60%;
}

#compact-confirm {
    width: 64;
    height: auto;
    background: #221F1A;
    border: round #3A3429;
    padding: 1 2;
}

#compact-confirm-title {
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
    padding-bottom: 1;
}

#compact-confirm-body {
    color: #D8CDBB;
}

/* Same border-box defect, same fix as #close-confirm-buttons above -- this
   is the one the operator actually hit (clicking the ctx% chip showed the
   dialog with no visible way out). See that rule's comment. */
#compact-confirm-buttons {
    height: auto;
    padding-top: 1;
}

#compact-confirm-buttons > Static {
    width: auto;
    padding-right: 2;
    color: #F2E9DD;
}

/* /about (item Z): the third member of the same modal family as
   CloseWithTurnRunning and CompactConfirm above -- identical wash, surface
   ramp, title treatment and button row. Wider (72) because its body is a
   two-column table of version strings and paths rather than a sentence,
   and a config path that wraps mid-path is a path nobody can paste. */
AboutDialog {
    align: center middle;
    background: #171512 60%;
}

#about {
    width: 72;
    height: auto;
    background: #221F1A;
    border: round #3A3429;
    padding: 1 2;
}

#about-title {
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
    padding-bottom: 1;
}

#about-body {
    color: #D8CDBB;
}

/* `height: auto`, for the third time and for the v0.28.0 reason spelled
   out on #close-confirm-buttons above: border-box means `height: 1` plus
   `padding-top: 1` leaves a 0-row content box and draws nothing at all.
   tests/test_about.py asserts this row's rendered height directly. */
#about-buttons {
    height: auto;
    padding-top: 1;
}

#about-buttons > Static {
    width: auto;
    padding-right: 2;
    color: #F2E9DD;
}

/* Inline tab rename (double-click a tab header): the field takes the
   tab's own slot in the strip -- one row high, no border of its own, so
   the strip does not jump while a name is being typed. */
#tab-rename {
    height: 1;
    border: none;
    padding: 0 1;
    background: #2A251E;
    color: #F2E9DD;
}

#block-list {
    background: $doxa-base;
    padding: 1 2;
}

/* TurnBlock: unboxed -- no border, no block-level fill of its own. The
   fold header (CollapsibleTitle, the user's prompt) is the ONE raised
   band, one step up from the screen; Contents (the response + tools
   below it) sits on the screen's own background, so the agent's reply
   reads as written directly on the surface rather than boxed on top of
   it. width:1fr on the title (Textual's own CollapsibleTitle defaults to
   width:auto, sized to its label) is deliberate -- without it the raised
   tint would only paint behind the prompt's own glyphs, not read as a
   full-width band. */
TurnBlock {
    background: $doxa-base;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

TurnBlock > CollapsibleTitle {
    width: 1fr;
    background: #221F1A;
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
    padding: 0 2;
}

TurnBlock > Contents {
    background: $doxa-base;
    padding: 1 0 0 2;
}

.turn-body {
    background: $doxa-base;
    color: #F2E9DD;
    padding: 0 1 1 1;
}

/* Reasoning (v0.25.0): same auto-height reasoning as .turn-tools below --
   zero rows with no ReasoningSection mounted, one row for a collapsed one.
   Sits ABOVE .turn-body in the widget tree (see TurnBlock.__init__), so
   reasoning always reads above the answer it led to. */
.turn-reasoning {
    height: auto;
    background: $doxa-base;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* Reasoning (N chars): doxa's own fold around the turn's summarized
   reasoning, same dimmer-step chrome as ToolCallsSection (chrome framing
   model prose, not a transcript entry of its own) -- collapsed by
   default, created lazily on the first reasoning_delta. */
ReasoningSection {
    background: #1D1B17;
    border: none;
    padding: 0 1;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

ReasoningSection > CollapsibleTitle {
    width: 1fr;
    background: #1D1B17;
    color: #8A8073;
    text-style: none;
    padding: 0 1;
}

ReasoningSection > Contents {
    background: #1D1B17;
    padding: 1 0 0 1;
}

.reasoning-body {
    background: #1D1B17;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    padding: 0 1 1 1;
}

.turn-tools {
    /* height:auto is load-bearing, not decoration: Vertical's own
       DEFAULT_CSS is height:1fr, which -- inside an auto-height Contents,
       itself inside a scrolling #block-list -- resolves against the
       viewport and stretches this container to fill it, padding every
       turn out to a full screen of empty space below its real content.
       That pre-existed the border removal (masked by the two extra
       border rows the old boxed TurnBlock spent on top/bottom edges);
       without the border to absorb the difference, the stretch was
       enough to scroll the turn's OWN title a row past the fold on a
       32-row terminal (measured while regenerating scripts/screenshot.py's
       gallery). auto makes this hug its content -- zero rows with no
       chips, one row for a collapsed ToolCallsSection -- like every
       other auto-height container in this file. */
    height: auto;
    background: $doxa-base;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* Tool calls (N): doxa's own wrapper around a turn's top-level chips
   (see doxa/app.py's ToolCallsSection), never model content -- so it
   gets the SAME dimmer-step + muted-text treatment as SystemBlock below,
   not a border. Created lazily, collapsed by default. Each chip inside
   keeps ToolChip's own bordered chrome untouched. */
ToolCallsSection {
    background: #1D1B17;
    border: none;
    padding: 0 1;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

ToolCallsSection > CollapsibleTitle {
    width: 1fr;
    background: #1D1B17;
    color: #8A8073;
    text-style: none;
    padding: 0 1;
}

ToolCallsSection > Contents {
    background: #1D1B17;
    padding: 1 0 0 1;
}

.tool-calls-list {
    background: #1D1B17;
    height: auto;
}

/* ToolChip is the deliberate exception to the unboxed rule above: a tool
   call reads as a nested artifact, not a transcript entry, and keeps its
   bordered chrome at every fold level of a trace tree (unchanged by
   v0.13.0 -- it lived inside .turn-tools before, it lives inside
   ToolCallsSection now). */
ToolChip {
    background: #2A251E;
    border: round #3A3429;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
    padding: 0 1;
}

ToolChip > CollapsibleTitle {
    color: #D8CDBB;
    text-style: none;
}

.chip-body {
    color: #D8CDBB;
    padding: 1 2;
}

/* Trace tree: a subagent's own tool calls nest as further chips inside
   their Task chip (indented one step), and its streamed text renders in
   the dimmed .chip-subout block -- both lazily, on expand. */
.chip-subcalls {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 0 0 2;
}

.chip-subout {
    color: #D8CDBB;
    padding: 0 2;
}

/* Image surfaces (doxa/images.py ladder): the media area inside a tool
   chip, the /img debug block, and the text fallback every tier degrades
   to. Sizes stay auto -- textual-image widgets measure themselves. */
.chip-media {
    height: auto;
}

.image-block {
    height: auto;
    background: #221F1A;
    border: round #3A3429;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

.image-caption {
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
}

.image-widget {
    height: auto;
    max-height: 24;
}

.image-fallback {
    color: #D8CDBB;
}

/* /img's report table and the per-tier labels above each rendered
   sample -- chrome around the pictures, so the muted system ramp. */
.image-diagnostics {
    color: #8A8073;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

.image-mode-label {
    color: #D97757;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* The opening banner (doxa/banner.py). Width is set on the widget in
   CELLS -- see BootBanner -- and height is deliberately auto so the image
   derives its rows from THIS terminal's real cell aspect rather than from
   an assumed 2:1. max-height is the row budget's backstop: a terminal
   reporting a wildly non-square cell may not spend half the budget again.
   No border, no background: the banner introduces the identity block
   below it and must not read as a block of its own. */
.boot-banner {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

.banner-image {
    height: auto;
    max-height: 9;
    max-width: 100%;
}

.banner-wordmark {
    height: 3;
}

.thinking {
    color: #D97757;
    height: 1;
}

/* System/TUI-internal lines (doxa-generated, not model-generated): the
   dimmer step on the ramp, one below the screen, with muted text -- the
   same tint ToolCallsSection uses for the same reason (chrome, not a
   transcript entry) and PeerMessageBlock already used for "another
   session, dimmer than this one's own turns" below. No border. */
.system-block {
    background: #1D1B17;
    color: #8A8073;
    border: none;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

/* Shell output (item Q, the `!` prefix): its own kind, and the styling is
   the requirement rather than a flourish -- the user must never confuse
   what a command printed with what the model said. So: no `▎` turn accent,
   no `▎ doxa` system prefix, but a green left rule (#7A9B6E) worn by
   NOTHING else in this app, and the terminal's own text color on the
   darkest surface. The block's own `❯` command line and its trailing exit
   code come from ShellBlock. */
ShellBlock {
    background: #17150F;
    color: #C9C0AE;
    border-left: thick #7A9B6E;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

/* Peer messages: deliberately dimmer than a turn -- border one step DOWN
   the surface ramp (#2A251E, the chip surface, instead of #3A3429) and
   secondary text only. Another session talking must never read as this
   session's own output. */
PeerMessageBlock {
    background: #1D1B17;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    border: round #2A251E;
    padding: 0 2;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}

#status-bar {
    background: #221F1A;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    height: 1;
    padding: 0 2;
}

/* Subagent tracker (queue item 4): the second status row, mounted only
   while at least one Task-spawned subagent is running (doxa/app.py's
   SessionPane._sync_subagent_line) -- same surface and text color as the
   status bar right above it, so the two read as one status area rather
   than a bar plus an unrelated strip. Each `⧉ label` span is a click
   target (SubagentLine.action_open_transcript); Textual paints its own
   link style on @click markup, so the hover/active affordance is free. */
#subagent-line {
    background: #221F1A;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    height: 1;
    padding: 0 2;
}

/* A subagent's own read-only transcript tab (SubagentTranscriptTab): same
   unboxed treatment as a turn's body -- this is trace material, not a
   turn of its own, so it reads directly on the surface. The narration
   line and the mirrored chip list share the one scrolling container. */
.transcript-scroll {
    background: $doxa-base;
    padding: 1 2;
}

.transcript-narration {
    color: #D8CDBB;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}

.transcript-chips {
    height: auto;
}

/* Slash autocomplete (doxa/app.py's SlashComplete): the dropdown above the
   prompt input. Same treatment as the palette's result list -- block
   surface, ramp-top border, Claude-orange on the highlighted row -- so the
   two command surfaces look like the one registry they read. Height is
   auto and capped: a dozen commands must never eat the transcript. */
#slash-complete {
    height: auto;
    max-height: 10;
    background: #221F1A;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    border: round #3A3429;
    margin: 0 1;
}

/* Group headers are disabled Options: dim, unselectable, and skipped by
   the highlight -- a label, never a destination. */
#slash-complete > .option-list--option-disabled {
    color: #8A8073;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* Ctrl+P palette: its section headers are the SAME disabled rows as the
   dropdown's group headers, so both command surfaces read alike. */
CommandList > .option-list--option-disabled {
    color: #8A8073;
    text-style: bold;
}

#slash-complete > .option-list--option-highlighted {
    color: #D97757;
    background: #2A251E;
    text-style: bold;
}

#prompt-input {
    background: #221F1A;
    color: #F2E9DD;
    border: round #D97757;
    margin: 0 1 1 1;
}

/* Chip picker (status-chips, item Y): the ONE shared dropdown every
   clickable status-bar chip opens -- model, branch, effort -- same
   block-surface/ramp-border/orange-highlight treatment as #slash-complete
   directly above, so the app reads as one dropdown language rather than
   two. Mounted in the SAME "above the prompt" slot the three prompt-
   driven popups use (doxa/app.py's compose()), which is also why it needs
   no extra CSS for its `:focus` border -- unlike those three (can_focus
   False, driven entirely through PromptInput's key protocol because
   typing owns focus at that point), THIS popup takes real focus the
   moment it opens (nothing else needs the caret while a chip menu is up),
   so OptionList's own DEFAULT_CSS `:focus` rule already applies. */
#chip-picker {
    height: auto;
    max-height: 10;
    background: #221F1A;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    border: round #3A3429;
    margin: 0 1;
}

#chip-picker > .option-list--option-disabled {
    color: #8A8073;
    text-style: bold;
}

#chip-picker > .option-list--option-highlighted {
    color: #D97757;
    background: #2A251E;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* Belief inspector -- Phase 2 stub pane (palette-toggled, hidden by
   default). Docked right so toggling never reflows the block list's
   scroll anchor; real content lands in Phase 3. Wears the house panel
   header: title plus a clickable close affordance. */
#belief-inspector {
    dock: right;
    display: none;
    width: 46;
    height: 1fr;
    background: #1D1B17;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    border: round #3A3429;
    padding: 1 2;
}

#inspector-header {
    height: 1;
}

#inspector-title {
    width: 1fr;
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
}

#inspector-body {
    color: #D8CDBB;
    padding-top: 1;
}

/* Command palette (Ctrl+P): Textual's built-in modal, reskinned onto the
   DOXA surface ramp -- dimmed screen behind (the 60% wash over the screen
   surface is the house modal-overlay treatment), block surface for the
   panel, Claude-orange for matched spans. */
CommandPalette {
    background: #171512 60%;
}

CommandPalette > Vertical {
    background: #221F1A;
}

CommandPalette #--input {
    background: #221F1A;
    border: round #D97757;
}

CommandPalette #--results {
    background: #221F1A;
}

CommandPalette CommandList {
    background: #221F1A;
    border: round #3A3429;
    color: #F2E9DD;
}

CommandPalette > .command-palette--help-text {
    color: #D8CDBB;
}

CommandPalette > .command-palette--highlight {
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* The live /search popup: same slab as the slash dropdown, directly above
   the prompt, never focused. Taller cap than the command dropdown because
   a result row carries a title, a date and a snippet. */
#session-search {
    height: auto;
    max-height: 12;
    background: #221F1A;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    border: round #3A3429;
    margin: 0 1;
}

#session-search > .option-list--option-disabled {
    color: #8A8073;
}

#session-search > .option-list--option-highlighted {
    background: #2A251E;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* The needs-input dialog (doxa/app.py's NeedsInputPopup, queue item 5):
   same slab and mount position as the slash dropdown and /search popup --
   an AskUserQuestion or a permission request the CLI would have prompted
   on both land here, above the prompt, via doxa.engine's can_use_tool
   callback. The heading row (the question, or the tool + input summary)
   wears the SAME red the tab's own -attention blink does, so the popup
   and the tab agree about what is asking for you. */
#needs-input-popup {
    height: auto;
    max-height: 12;
    background: #221F1A;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    border: round #B23B32;
    margin: 0 1;
}

#needs-input-popup > .option-list--option-disabled {
    color: #F2E9DD;
    background: #B23B32;
    text-style: bold;
}

#needs-input-popup > .option-list--option-highlighted {
    color: #D97757;
    background: #2A251E;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* Settings (Ctrl+, / /settings): same modal treatment again -- dimmed
   screen wash, block surface, ramp-top border. Rows are label / field /
   help, deliberately plain: nothing in this panel animates. */
SettingsScreen {
    align: center middle;
    background: #171512 60%;
}

#settings-panel {
    width: 80%;
    max-width: 100;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 90%;
    background: #221F1A;
    border: round #3A3429;
    padding: 1 2;
}

#settings-header {
    height: 1;
}

#settings-title {
    width: 1fr;
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* Panel close affordance: the ✕ every closeable panel in the house wears
   in its upper-right corner, for the mouse users the key bindings leave
   out. Static, not a Button -- no ripple, no transition, no timer. */
.panel-close {
    width: 3;
    content-align: center middle;
    color: #D8CDBB;
    text-style: bold;
}

.panel-close:hover {
    color: #D97757;
}

#settings-categories {
    height: auto;
    max-height: 30;
}

#settings-categories ContentTabs {
    background: #221F1A;
    color: #D8CDBB;
}

#settings-categories Tab.-active {
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
}

#settings-categories Underline > .underline--bar {
    color: #D97757;
    background: #3A3429;
}

.settings-rows {
    height: auto;
    max-height: 24;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* The effective value, and where it came from -- the two things a
   settings row exists to tell you. */
.setting-value {
    color: #D97757;
    padding: 0 1;
}

.setting-shadowed {
    color: #8A8073;
    padding: 0 1;
}

.setting-note {
    color: #8A8073;
    padding: 0 1 1 1;
}

.setting-label {
    color: #F2E9DD;
    text-style: bold;
}

.setting-field {
    background: #1D1B17;
    color: #F2E9DD;
    border: round #3A3429;
}

.setting-field:focus {
    border: round #D97757;
}

.setting-readonly {
    color: #D8CDBB;
    padding: 0 1;
}

.setting-help {
    color: #8A8073;
    padding: 0 1 1 1;
}

#settings-path {
    color: #8A8073;
    padding-top: 1;
}

#settings-hint {
    color: #D8CDBB;
}

/* /setup: same modal treatment as settings and the close-confirm --
   dimmed wash, block surface, ramp-top border. One finding at a time,
   plain text, nothing animated. */
SetupScreen {
    align: center middle;
    background: #171512 60%;
}

#setup-panel {
    width: 74;
    max-width: 90%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 80%;
    background: #221F1A;
    border: round #3A3429;
    padding: 1 2;
}

#setup-header {
    height: 1;
}

#setup-title {
    width: 1fr;
    color: #D97757;
    text-style: bold;
}

#setup-body {
    color: #D8CDBB;
    padding: 1 0;
}

#setup-hint {
    color: #D8CDBB;
}
